#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:140
msgid "Search Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:142
msgid "The query string consists of *terms* and *operators*. A term can be a single word or a phrase surrounded by double quotes. Operators allow to customize the search."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:149
msgid "Single word"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:147
msgid "If the query string is a single word (for example ``quick`` or ``brown``), then OTOBO searches for all items containing the given word."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:149
msgid "If two or more words are given in the query string (for example ``quick brown``), then OTOBO searches for all items containing the word ``quick`` **or** ``brown``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:152
msgid "Phrase surrounded by double quotes"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:152
msgid "If the query string contains a phrase surrounded by double quotes (for example ``\"quick brown\"``), then OTOBO searches for all items containing the words in the phrase in the same order."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:159
msgid "Wildcards"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:155
msgid "The question mark ``?`` replaces a single character, and the asterisk ``*`` replaces zero or more characters (for example ``qu?ck bro*``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:159
msgid "Wildcard queries can lead to performance issues because many terms need to be queried to match the query string."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:166
msgid "Regular expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:162
msgid "Regular expression patterns can be embedded in the query string by wrapping them in slashes (for example ``/joh?n(ath[oa]n)/``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:166
msgid "The supported regular expression syntax is explained in `Regular expression syntax <https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/query-dsl-regexp-query.html#regexp-syntax>`__ chapter of the Elasticsearch documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:174
msgid "Fuzziness"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:169
msgid "It is possible to search for terms that are similar to, but not exactly like the given search terms, using the *fuzzy* operator (for example ``quikc~ brwn~ foks~``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:171
msgid "The default fuzziness level is 2, but a level 1 should be sufficient to detect about 80% of misspellings. It can be specified as ``quikc~1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:174
msgid "Fuzziness can be disabled with ``quikc~0`` which will not consider spelling errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:180
msgid "Proximity"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:177
msgid "A query string like ``\"quick fox\"`` searches the words in exactly the same order, but the proximity search allows some other words to be included between the given words (for example ``\"fox quick\"~5``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:179
msgid "This operator specifies the maximum edit distance of words. The phrase *quick fox* would be considered more relevant than *quick brown fox*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:184
msgid "Ranges"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:183
msgid "The query string can contain ranges for date, numeric or string fields. Inclusive ranges are specified with square brackets ``[min TO max]`` and exclusive ranges are specified with curly brackets ``{min TO max}``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:190
msgid "Boosting"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:187
msgid "The *boost* operator ``^`` can be used to make one term more relevant than another. For example, the query string ``quick^2 fox``, finds all documents about foxes, but with special interest in quick foxes."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:190
msgid "Boosts can also be used for phrases or groups, for example ``\"quick fox\"^2 AND (brown lazy)^4``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:205
msgid "Boolean operators"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:193
msgid "The query string ``quick brown fox`` searches for all items containing one or more of the specified words."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:195
msgid "The preferred operators are ``+`` (term must be present) and ``-`` (term must not be present). All other terms are optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:198
msgid "For example if the query string is ``quick brown +fox -news`` then it means:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:200
msgid "``fox`` must be present."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:201
msgid "``news`` must not be present."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:202
msgid "``quick`` and ``brown`` are optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:204
msgid "The well known logical operators ``AND``, ``OR`` and ``NOT`` (or ``&&``, ``||`` and ``!``) are also supported. The query string ``((quick AND fox) OR (brown AND fox) OR fox) AND NOT news`` is identical with the previous example."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:208
msgid "Grouping"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:208
msgid "Changing the precedence with parentheses is possible, as in ``(quick OR brown) AND fox``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:215
msgid "Reserved characters"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:211
msgid "There are some reserved characters which function as operators, and they can not be used in search queries. These reserved characters are: ``+ - = && || > < ! ( ) { } [ ] ^ \" ~ * ? : \\ /``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:214
msgid "If any of these characters need to be used in search queries, then they must be escapes with a leading backslash. For example to search for the term *(1+1)=2*, it is required the query string ``\\(1\\+1\\)\\=2``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/agent/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.rst:219
msgid "More information can be found in the `Query string syntax <https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/query-dsl-query-string-query.html#query-string-syntax>`__ chapter of the Elasticsearch documentation."
msgstr ""

